{{--
    A DELIBERATELY WRONG BLADE TEMPLATE. `QRPAGER-46`, for `R-267`.

    This file is the negative proof that `maskBladeCss()` + `scanSource()` actually read CSS
    out of a Blade template. `R-267` was open for a day with THREE scanners reporting clean on
    the real file, and the row's own instruction is that the fix is *"a reader, with its own
    tests and its own negative fixture"* — so every shape below is here on purpose, and
    `rule.test.mjs` asserts the EXACT count and the EXACT rule names rather than `> 0`.

    It is never rendered and never served. `isExempt()` covers
    `tools/design-tokens/fixtures`, and the name ends in `.fixture` so the Blade walk cannot
    reach it either — both halves are asserted, because an exemption is also how a fixture
    stops being proof of anything.

    ⚠ A HEX IN THIS COMMENT IS NOT A FINDING: #f5f5f5, and that is itself under test.
--}}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<style>
/* ---- what MUST be reported: every shape, inside the style element ---- */
.bad-colour{color:#ff00ff}
/* ⚠ `teal;` AND NOT `rebeccapurple}`. The first version of this line wrote the latter and
   fired NOTHING: the named-colour pattern carries a closed list of the colours that actually
   get typed by hand, and it requires the value to END the declaration (`;`, `!`, `)` or a
   line break) so that prose and identifiers are untouched. A fixture that exercises a pattern
   has to be written to the pattern's own shape, or it proves the pattern is absent. */
.bad-named{background:teal;}
.bad-function{border-color:rgba(16,35,27,0.06)}
.bad-spacing{padding:13px}
.bad-size{font-size:17px}

/* ---- what MUST NOT be reported, each for its own reason ---- */

{{--
    ⚠ A BLADE COMMENT INSIDE THE STYLE ELEMENT, QUOTING BOTH TAGS — which is the measured
    defect this fixture exists to keep closed. `page.blade.php`'s own comment says
    *"NO NEWLINE BEFORE `</style>`"* and *"no `<style`, no CSS rule"*, and with comments left
    in place the first version of the masker found TWO style elements in that one file: one
    ending at the `</style>` above, one opening at the `<style` above, with `[^>]*>` running
    to the next `>` in the CSS. Its finding list looked right by accident.

    The prose here also names literals — #f5f5f5, padding:13px, font-size:17px — and none of
    them is a finding, because an explanation of a literal is not a literal.
--}}

/* An echoed value is PHP, not CSS: the hex inside the braces never reaches the page as
   typed, and reporting it would be reporting the template's source. */
.echoed{color:{{ '#ff00ff' }}}

@php
    // A PHP block inside a style element. Wrong, but if it ever happens its contents are
    // PHP and the hex below is a PHP string rather than a declaration.
    $colour = '#ff00ff';
@endphp

/* Blade control flow around real rules. The DIRECTIVES are blanked and the rules between
   them are still read -- `.in-branch` below is a finding and is counted. */
@if ($template === 'rail')
.in-branch{padding:11px}
@elseif ($template === 'ember')
.in-other-branch{color:var(--color-text)}
@endif

/* ⚠ `@media` IS CSS AND MUST SURVIVE THE BLANKER. A blanker that removed every `@word`
   would hide every declaration inside an at-rule, which is where a responsive literal
   hides. The padding below is a finding. */
@media (min-width:48rem){.in-media{padding:19px}}
</style>
</head>
<body>
{{-- Outside the style element: this scanner's subject is a `<style>` body, and an inline
     attribute is `scan-panel.mjs`'s. Neither of the two below is a finding HERE, and the
     test says so rather than leaving it to be assumed. --}}
<p style="color:#ff00ff;padding:13px">A literal in an attribute.</p>
<p>And one in prose: #abcdef, padding:13px.</p>
</body>
</html>
